# API Authentication

## Identity + cookies

- ASP.NET Identity (`ApplicationUser`, `IdentityRole`)
- Application cookie (`Auth:CookieName`, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax)
- Antiforgery cookie + `X-CSRF-TOKEN` on authenticated mutations
- Global roles seeded: protected `Admin` and `Member`

## AuthController

| Endpoint | Auth | Notes |
|----------|------|-------|
| `GET /api/v1/auth/csrf` | Anonymous | Returns request token |
| `POST /api/v1/auth/login` | Anonymous | Cookie session; rate limited |
| `POST /api/v1/auth/logout` | `[Authorize]` | Signs out; CSRF required |
| `GET /api/v1/auth/me` | `[Authorize]` | User, roles, permissions |
| `PUT /api/v1/auth/profile` | `[Authorize]` | Update profile; CSRF required |
| `POST /api/v1/auth/change-password` | `[Authorize]` | Change password; CSRF required |

## Password / lockout

Configured in `AddEnterprisePlatform`: minimum 12 characters, complexity, lockout after 5 failures for 15 minutes.

## Seed

`InitializeEnterpriseDatabaseAsync` seeds roles and an optional bootstrap admin. Bootstrap, created, and reset users receive temporary passwords and `MustChangePassword=true`.

There is no public registration endpoint. Authorized admins manage users through `/api/v1/users`.

## Authorization

Roles contain `permission` claims. Policies check claims, not role names. RBAC is global to the application. Module permissions join the catalog at compile time.

## Do not

- Return password hashes in DTOs
- Reintroduce JWT bearer as the default browser session model
- Add registration without a deliberate product/security decision
- Skip CSRF for authenticated state-changing cookie requests
- Authorize product behavior by role name instead of permission policy
